saudi arabia's crown prince mohammed bin  salman is visiting turkey for the first  time since the murder of a saudi  journalist plunged relations between the  two countries into crisis the crown  prince  today met with turkish president recep  tayyip erdogan in ankara both men say  that they want to mend ties that have  been strained since  journalist jamal khashoggi was murdered  in the saudi consulate in istanbul back  in 2018.  now you may remember that u.s  intelligence concluded that the salty  crown prince probably ordered the murder  of khashoggi in 2018 turkish president  erdogan blamed khashoggi's murder on the  highest levels of the saudi government  and he launched a global campaign  against the kingdom's leadership now  he's calling the crown prince his friend  so why is erdogan so desperate to  normalize relations here's dw  correspondent dorian jones in istanbul  well it's necessity turkey is facing  regional isolation having effectively  upset nearly every leader in the region  and this is part of a wider policy of  resetting those relations we've seen a  similar approach monitors with the  united arab emirates with egypt and  israel but in many ways saudi arabia is  seen as key this is not only a regional  power but turkey is looking for its  financial support president erdogan is  in charge of an economy that is in  crisis inflation is officially eighty  percent but most believe it's well over  a hundred percent and the and the  currency the turkish lira is under siege  with warnings it could face a further  crash and erdogan is believed to be  looking for major financial support from  saudi crown prince bin salman  to uh tie over its economy until next  year's elections where erdogan faces  re-election  that was dorian jones reporting there my  next guest tonight was closely connected  to the work of jamal khashoggi  karen atiyah was khashoggi's editor at  the washington post where she herself is  a columnist karen is also the author of  say your word then leave the  assassination of jamal khashoggi in the  power of the truth that book due out  next month karen joins me tonight from  dallas texas karen it's good to see you  again um  there's a lot to talk about here but  first i just want to get your reaction  to  the this visit by muhammad bin salman to  turkey today  yeah i mean  what is there to say  except  this is  something that  he feared obviously from the  um the moment we heard that dama uh had  been  killed um  just we always wondered um you know from  our purchase at in washington you know  just how far was turkey really willing  to go  uh for somebody um who yes was close to  erdogan but uh you know saudi arabia you  know as your report rightly said being a  a huge power in the region  you know it's it's just it was just a  matter  perhaps of time i mean that's the  cynical look at it but um what i think  about right now  is just the  horrific message um that this uh  you know um new renewed friendship sends  to the dissidents to the um journalists  to those who have uh found turkey to be  um or istanbul to be a place uh that  they can gather and gather during my  reporting i met with a good amount of  um  arab saudis who had fled to istanbul  because they felt it was safe and now  basically the message is out you know  you can be killed  uh kidnapped and eventually wait a few  years uh  and  you know nothing will happen  you look good we've heard arguments  that  realities have to take president here  that it it's it's time to to move on and  get on with the business of geopolitics  is it  yeah this is something i  addressed in in my latest column for the  post  there's this  idea right that you have geopolitics you  have so-called realism  um you know another thing to consider  with saudi arabia particularly when it  comes to the us is the us is looking at  saudi arabia to be a partner with  trying to normalize relations with  israel and so you always have this uh  these arguments that say well we need to  balance human rights with freedom  uh sorry with national security and with  realism and i think i would argue to  think that this is separate from  geopolitics to think that that basically  um journalists  uh anyone who speaks up um that that is  not a matter of of  national security or not or that that is  not a matter that um  really frankly leads to  insecurity i think it's quite  unimaginative frankly  in your piece today in the washington  post  you ask will president u.s president  biden push our allies to respect press  freedom when he visits saudi arabia next  month for example and you answer that  with  one sentence i'm not holding my breath  why  i mean uh  jamal's case uh jamal was also a u.s  resident right  um and i think that you know from the  reporting we're seeing here you know as  you said  it's looking like administ the  administration as well officials within  the administration here are saying well  this relationship can't be um held  hostage to to the khashoggi murder but  i've long said that this what happened  to jamal was more than just what  happened to him it was also an awakening  to saudi arabia's actions in yemen to  the  u.s support of the war in yemen and it  really was  not a bump in the road it was a tipping  point in questioning um u.s pretty much  unconditional support um for its allies  regardless of who gets killed so it it  sends the message against west  journalists as well  that we are not safe if we happen to be  targeted by one of our allies  in the middle east  your new book karen is about  this murder and  the power of the truth and i'm wondering  what do you say when someone asks you  you know what condition is the truth in  in this case khashoggi's family for  example they're still waiting for  justice aren't they  um and we  we all are there was a reason why um his  case uh his situation was so  uh in a ways you know we weren't just  fighting for him but again fighting for  um all the activists all of the  journalists who  um who jamal even himself uh stood for  in writing in his columns in in the  washington post  so you know to  um i i think you know obviously  we're not going to let you know my  government or the government of saudi  arabia or turkey um forget uh about  their actions um in this particular case  because it is um so much about um just  how far  uh countries and governments are willing  to go  uh to maintain relations and to preserve  their power and i don't think that is  something that um we can never forget  yeah and that's what we are trying to  make sure  does not happen for sure  karen t as always karen we appreciate  your time and your insights tonight we  look forward to your new book as well  thank you  thank you so much
 

